{"cuisine/curacao-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/curacao-cuisine","n":"Curaçao cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Curaçao","intro":[["Curaçao, officially the Country of Curaçao, is a constituent country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands. It is an island country located in the southern Caribbean Sea, specifically the Dutch Caribbean region, about 65 km (40 mi) north of Venezuela and 80 km (50 mi) southeast of Aruba. Curaçao includes the main island of Curaçao, and the much smaller, uninhabited island of Klein Curaçao (\"Little Curaçao\")."],["The 2023 census recorded a population of nearly 156,000 people in Curaçao. The country has an area of 444 km2 (171 sq mi); its capital is Willemstad. Together with Aruba and Bonaire, Curaçao forms part of the ABC islands. Collectively, Curaçao, Aruba, and other Dutch islands in the Caribbean are often called the Dutch Caribbean. It is the largest of the ABC islands in terms of both area and population."],["The island is known for its indigenous bitter orange-based liqueur, Curaçao, flavoured with the dried peel of the laraha, a citrus fruit grown on the island."],["Curaçao was originally inhabited by the Arawak and Caquetío Indigenous Peoples. The first Europeans to explore the island were Spanish, who first reached it in 1499. The island became a Spanish colony after Alonso de Ojeda's 1499 expedition. Though labelled \"the Useless Island\" due to its poor agricultural yield and lack of precious metals, it later became a strategic cattle ranching area. When the Dutch colonized the island in 1634, they shifted the island's focus to trade and shipping, and later made it a hub of the Atlantic slave trade."],["British forces occupied Curaçao twice during the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars, but it was returned to Dutch rule. The abolition of slavery in 1863 led to economic shifts and migrations. Dutch remains the official language, though Papiamento, English, and Spanish are widely spoken, reflecting the island's diverse cultural influences. Curaçao was part of the Curaçao and Dependencies colony from 1815 to 1954, and part of the Netherlands Antilles from 1954 until 2010, as Island Territory of Curaçao."],["The discovery of oil in the Maracaibo Basin in 1914 transformed Curaçao into a major refinery location, altering its economic landscape. There were efforts towards becoming a country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands; the island achieved autonomy in 2010."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"curacao-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curaçao#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"This Is the Way We Cook! Asina Nos Ta Cushina","ref":"cookbook/this-is-the-way-we-cook-asina-nos-ta-cushina","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jewell Fenzi","note":"Recipes from home cooks of Aruba, Bonaire, and Curaçao.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/zante-currant":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/zante-currant","n":"Zante currant","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Zante currants, Corinth raisins, Corinthian raisins or simply currants, are raisins of the small, sweet, seedless grape cultivar Black Corinth (Vitis vinifera). The name comes from the Anglo-French phrase \"raisins de Corinthe\" (grapes of Corinth) and the Ionian island of Zakynthos (Zante), which was once the major producer and exporter. It is not related to black, red or white currants, which are berries of shrubs in the genus Ribes and not usually prepared in dried form."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-zante-currant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zante_currant"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"]]],["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"]]],["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine",[["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Currant","ingredient/currant"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Nutmeg","ingredient/nutmeg"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Grape","ingredient/grape"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}]},"dish/chicken-paprikash":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chicken-paprikash","n":"Chicken paprikash","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Chicken paprikash or paprika chicken is a popular Hungarian cuisine dish and one of the most famous variations of the paprikás preparations common to Hungarian tables."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chicken-paprikash"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicken_paprikash"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine"],["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Vienna Sunday Kitchen","ref":"site/vienna-sunday-kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vienna-sunday.kitchen","note":"Viennese food blog of Austrian home cooking and pastries, with English-language recipes.","url":"https://www.vienna-sunday.kitchen/newest-recipes/","suggested":true},{"name":"Lil Vienna","ref":"site/lilvienna-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lilvienna.com","note":"Austrian 